_:genid179 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> _:genid180 .
_:genid180 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
_:genid180 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#intersectionOf> _:genid183 .
_:genid183 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:genid181 .
_:genid181 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:genid181 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> _:genid182 .
_:genid182 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#someValuesFrom> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000015> .
_:genid182 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000056> .
_:genid182 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
_:genid181 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#List> .
_:genid183 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000001> .
_:genid183 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#List> .
_:genid179 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000052> .
_:genid179 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000005> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000114> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000122> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000005> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000117> "PERSON: Joerg Waitelonis" .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000005> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A property is a material trait in terms of the kind and magnitude of response to a specific imposed stimulus. Generally, definitions of properties are made independent of material shape and size.\n(Callister, W.D., Rethwisch, D.G., Materials Science and Engineering, Wiley, 2014)\n\nTechnical materials are complex aggregates. Many properties that are determined for those aggregates are intrinsically associated with the methodologies employed in the measurement process. This is in contrast to typical 'physical' properties, which like e.g. mass that can be determined to high accuracy independently of the measurement process. \nAs a consequence, material properties in the ontology are differeciated according to their measurement method, e.g. Brinell hardness and Vickers hardness are different types of indentation hardness rather than different measures/quantification (GDC) of the materials property 'indentation hardness'.\n The 'unit idenifieres' of such properties (e.g. \"HV1\", \"HV10\", \"HBW\") are strictly speaking not units but rather references to the measurement methodology used to determine a numeric value that quantifies the property.\n\nExtensive properties that depend on the object being tested rather than on the portion of matter are (extensive) object- or system-properties."@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000005> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Materialeigenschaft"@de .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000005> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "material performance property"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000005> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "a disposition of a portion of matter that is realized in a compatible process and whose realization is grounded in the portions intensive qualities"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000005> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000060> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
_:genid184 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Axiom> .
_:genid184 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#annotatedSource> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000005> .
_:genid184 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#annotatedProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> .
_:genid184 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#annotatedTarget> _:genid179 .
_:genid184 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Due to the duality of object and portion of matter this axiom can not be an equivalent class axiom."@en .
