# https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020154
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020154> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020154> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020136> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020154> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "It proceeds by nucleation and growth."@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020154> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "precipitation (phase)"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020154> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Precipitation from a supersaturated solid solution is a phase transformation process in which a single supersaturated parent phase decomposes into a solute‑depleted matrix and dispersed, compositionally distinct precipitate phases."@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020154> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000060> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
# 
# https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020155
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020155> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020155> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020142> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020155> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000114> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000122> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020155> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000117> "PERSON: Philipp von Hartrott" .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020155> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "interatomare Wechselwirkungsenergie"@de .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020155> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "interatomic interaction energy"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020155> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Interatomare Wechselwirkungsenergie wird dem Atomaggregat hinzugefügt/entfernt, wenn ein Atom unendlich vom Rest des Aggregats entfernt wird."@de .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020155> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Interatomic interaction energy is added/removed from the atom aggregate if one atom is moved infinitely away from the rest of the aggregate."@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020155> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000060> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
# 
# https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020156
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020156> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020156> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020136> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020156> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "It is marked on phase diagrams by a peritectic point and proceeds by an interfacial, diffusion‑controlled reaction that is often kinetically sluggish, leading to incomplete transformation or complex microstructures during solidification."@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020156> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "peritectic phase transformation"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020156> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A peritectic phase transformation is a phase transformation in which a liquid parent phase and an existing solid phase react on cooling to form a different solid phase (L + α → β)."@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020156> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000060> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
# 
# https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020157
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020157> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020157> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020136> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020157> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Unlike nucleation and growth transformations, it has no nucleation barrier and proceeds by uphill diffusion and wavelength‑selective amplification of composition modulations, producing an interconnected, compositionally modulated microstructure that coarsens over time."@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020157> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "spinodal decomposition"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020157> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Spinodal decomposition is a diffusion‑controlled  continuous phase transformation in which a single homogeneous solution spontaneously separates into two compositionally distinct phases by amplification of infinitesimal concentration fluctuations."@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020157> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000060> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
# 
