<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0080094> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000116> "Necessary and sufficient conditions for 'stress relaxation' have not yet been formalized. The term is defined as a specific subtype of 'physical relational quality'. Distinguishing criteria are domain-specific and depend on measurement conventions not yet expressed as OWL restrictions."@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0080094> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000117> "PERSON: Thomas Hanke" .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0080094> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Spannungsrelaxation"@de .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0080094> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "stress relaxation"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0080094> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Relaxationsgrad"@de .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0080094> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "relaxation ratio"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0080094> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Stress relaxation is a physical relational quality of a material that expresses the ratio of residual stress to initial applied stress after a defined time interval under constant strain, quantifying the material's tendency to internally redistribute stress over time"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0080094> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000060> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
