<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090022> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "resin role"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090022> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#comment> "Typical bearers of this role include curable or processable materials used in polymer formulation, coating, impregnation, adhesive application, or composite manufacturing. Many such bearers are viscous liquids or solids that may later be transformed into higher-molar-mass, networked materials or a polymeric matrix during processing." .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090022> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A resin role is a role borne by a material entity that is realized when the bearer functions as a matrix-forming, binding, adhesive, coating, or impregnating component in a material processing or manufacturing process." .
# 
# https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090023
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090023> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090023> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0010034> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090023> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "curing (polymers)"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090023> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#comment> "In many thermosetting systems, polymer curing involves crosslinking and leads to formation of a hardened network. \nPolymer curing results in an increase in molar mass, viscosity, and structural stability. \nWhile crosslinking is a primary mechanism for thermosets, physical curing or chain extension mechanisms can also occur." .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090023> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A polymer curing process is a curing process in which a monomeric, oligomeric, or polymeric precursor undergoes chemical reaction to form a polymer network or material of higher molar mass."@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090024
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090024> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090024> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000015> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090024> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "crosslinking"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090024> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "cross-linking"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090024> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#comment> "Crosslinking can involve covalent bonds, ionic interactions, or other linking interactions between polymer chains.\nIt can reduce solubility and melt flow while increasing thermal and mechanical stability." .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090024> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A crosslinking process is a chemical or physical interaction process in which chemical bonds or other linking interactions are formed between macromolecular chains or distinct regions of a polymeric material, thereby increasing network connectivity or contributing to network formation." .
# 
# https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090050
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090050> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090050> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0050022> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090050> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "thermosetting polyurethane"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090050> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#comment> "offers high versatility in mechanical properties" .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090050> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "thermosetting polymer with carbamate crosslinks"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090050> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0050117> "PUR" .
# 
# https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090051
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090051> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090051> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0050022> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090051> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "unsaturated polyester resins"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090051> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#comment> "they are used for fiber-reinforced composites" .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090051> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "thermosetting polymer system of unsaturated polyester prepolymers in reactive vinyl monomers; it cures by radical crosslinking into rigid networks"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090052
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090052> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090052> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0050032> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090052> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "silicone rubber"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090052> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#comment> "it is available in multiple formulations and often filled." .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090052> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#comment> "wide service temperature range" .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090052> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "synthetic rubber that belongs to crosslinked polysiloxane elastomer family, with organic side groups"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090052> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "widely used for its broad service temperature range in medical devices, gaskets, and culinary tools" .
# 
# https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090053
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090053> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090053> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0050032> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090053> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "polychloropren"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090053> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> " Neoprene" .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090053> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#comment> "it offers good weathering and ozone resistance." .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090053> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#comment> "weathering resistance; ozone resistance" .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090053> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "synthetic rubber that belongs to the family of synthetic elastomeric rubbers; synthesised typically through emulsion polymerization of chloroprene"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0090053> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> " it is widely used in gaskets and wetsuits. " .
# 
# https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0200000
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0200000> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0200000> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000907> .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0200000> <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/source> "Official definition can be found in: DIN 8580, group 1.2.1 “Pressformen”."@de .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0200000> <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/source> "Offizielle Definition findet man in: DIN 8580, Gruppe 1.2.1 „Pressformen“."@de .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0200000> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Pressformen"@de .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0200000> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "compression molding"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0200000> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "compression moulding"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0200000> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A primary shaping from the plastic state process in which a pre-measured, usually preheated molding compound is placed in an open, heated mold cavity and shaped by closing the mold and applying pressure until the material cures or solidifies to the final part geometry."@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0200000> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Ein Urformverfahren, bei dem eine dosierte, meist vorgewärmte Formmasse in eine offene, beheizte Formkavität eingelegt und durch Schließen des Werkzeugs unter Druck zur endgültigen Bauteilgeometrie ausgehärtet bzw. erstarrt wird."@de .
